The above testing methods are based on Contracts which tests the methods based on different conditions and assumptions that are tested.

The issue which I am trying to resolve is with the PEX generated tests. The generated test cases are not executing in the way we think they should.

For example,

When we tested the `PUT_AddContract`, `PUT_RemoveContract` and `PUT_SetContract`, every test ended up with a condition failure (i.e., `{Contract Failed}`). I then discovered that these methods were not in fact contracts but methods to add and remove items. When these contracts were corrected to methods, the condition failures resolved.

The same thing happened with other contracts as well.

Another issue I observed, was something like "`Contract Violation at Line No`". In some of the contracts I was testing, I got this error message. I'm not sure how to interpret this error message.
Could someone please help me interpreting this and guide me on how to tackle these issues?

A: Your tests are failing because Pex is trying to use the contracts you provided as a way of explaining what should happen when the method is called, but it isn't interpreting them correctly. The contract you need to use for the Add method is specifying that it should add a value to the list, but in reality it's throwing an exception.
The last part of your question - 'Contract Violation at Line No' - is somewhat irrelevant because it's a generic Pex framework message and not specific to your situation. It seems you have contract violations because the return value for your method doesn't match what the contract specified should happen. If a method returns 'void', Pex doesn't check return values in contracts.
So, what you need to do is re-think your contracts:


*

*Check that your method's contracts accurately describe what your method does. 

*Make sure the methods you are calling (such as Add, Contains, IndexOf, et cetera) have the appropriate contracts as well.

*If you can't figure out how to write your own contracts, consider adding pre and post conditions around your method.

*If a method (like Add) is being tested and it's not behaving as you expect, it may be returning something other than what you specified in the contract.

*Pex contracts are essentially documentation, they should accurately describe what your method is supposed to do.


Also, this part of your question is somewhat open-ended and it seems like it might be a separate issue with your Pex test setup. If you're seeing something like Contract Violation at Line Number, you should try checking your setup to ensure that Pex is correctly instrumenting your code and that you're using the contracts as intended.

A: The issue you are facing may be due to the order in which Pex tests input parameters. Pex normally will run the test in random order but you can control it using the TestOrder attribute.
Consider adding this to your test method:
[TestOrder(100)]

Also, ensure all the constraints, preconditions, and assert conditions are properly written. Be sure all your conditions are correctly associated with the associated Pex method.
[PexMethod] attributes are used to mark certain methods as being testable by Pex. Ensure your methods are correctly marked. And, make sure that you have properly specified return, parameter and invariant contracts.
Furthermore, in Pex testing, keep in mind that the contract specified for method (what method SHOULD return, input parameters, invariant property) should be in line with actual code behavior which is under test. Pex tries to test contracts but if you put the wrong method here, you may end up with condition passes even if the actual code is not being tested as expected.
Lastly, it's always recommended to have a good understanding of the actual code you are testing, and how Pex will test it to minimize the number of condition failures.
These tips could help troubleshoot the issues you are having. If this doesn't resolve your issue please provide more specific details about what's wrong and the code you are testing.
