A: The problem here is the fact that the PexClass is on the TestFixture class itself instead of on the specific test methods. 
So you need to annotate your PexMethod (like your AddContract method) with [PexMethod] instead of having the [PexClass] on the TestFixture. Also remember that each [PexMethod] needs to be public.
Here is how I see your unit test class rewritten:

[TestFixture]
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
    {
     //...
    }

    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
    {
     //...
    }

    //And so on...
}

Hope it helps, good luck with your testing using Pex :) 

A: I can't comment due to limitations but I can share some insights on the error. Could it be possible that you might not have included all the required namespaces in your test file? The main namespaces for ArrayList should be System.Collections and System.Collections.Generic.

A: Here's the error:

The "Index" is out of range. Must be within the range of valid indices in the list. (Parameter 'index')

This error occurs because one of the test parameters index is greater than the count of the ArrayList instance arrList.
This is causing your IndexOutOfRangeException at this line.
You just added an element (x) into the list but haven't increased the size of the ArrayList (arrList).
Try to initialize your arrList instance with at least one item before you start testing. You should increase size of your list before or during initialization. 
For example:
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
   arrList.Add(1); // fill with some value before removing
   // the rest of your code
}

You should apply the same technique to your other PUT_...Contract methods.
If you can't add elements to the array during initialization, you should add it in some initial arrangement of your test methods before you test the methods which remove elements from the arrList.
Also, I notice that you have an issue with the naming of your test methods. The Convention to follow is the Naming Convention for Unit Test Methods in C#. They should begin with the Verb (e.g., Get, Set, Add, Remove or something like Test, AddRemove etc.). So, it would be worthwhile to rename some of your test methods too.
I hope this may help to solve the problem. If not, please provide more details about your code.
Note: I mentioned that this could cause issues if you have no items in your ArrayList at all.

A: It looks like you are testing methods that modify the ArrayList. When you create an ArrayList using any method (like new), it is empty. Therefore, when you try to perform an operation like Remove() or Insert() on an index that doesn't exist, you're getting an IndexOutOfRangeException. 
Try to add items to your list before performing these operations:
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    arrList.Add(x); // Adding a value before removing
    // The rest of your code
}

Also remember, the naming of your test methods is important. The test method names should be as descriptive as possible. That way, when you (or someone else) sees the name of the test method, they can understand what exactly it tests.

